// The ONE answer to "which token is the cursor on", and a file of its own because TWO features read it.
// `textDocument/hover` renders what a position names and `textDocument/definition` jumps to where that
// name is declared; a second resolver would let an editor jump to a name the hover on that very position
// describes as something else. It sits apart from `LspSymbols.maxon`'s document-structure model because
// it answers about a POSITION rather than about the document's blocks.

/// No token lies at the offset asked about. ⭐ A THROW rather than an index standing for "none": a real
/// token at index 0 and "there is none" would otherwise be the same answer.
module enum LspCursorLookupError implements Error
	nothingThere
end 'LspCursorLookupError'

/// ⭐⭐ **WHICH TOKEN THE CURSOR NAMES — THE ONE ANSWER `textDocument/hover` AND `textDocument/definition`
/// BOTH READ.** Two resolvers would let an editor jump to a name the hover on that very position
/// describes as something else.
///
/// ⭐ **A CARET IS A POINT BETWEEN CHARACTERS, SO AT A BOUNDARY IT TOUCHES TWO TOKENS AND THE LEFT ONE
/// WINS.** One past the end of a name is where an editor leaves the caret the moment that name is typed —
/// and it is also where `.`, a `///` comment or a `#if` begins. The token that BEGINS there has not been
/// reached yet; the name just typed is what the user is pointing at. Restricted to an identifier, because
/// a caret past a `)` or past a keyword is past an expression rather than on a name.
///
/// ⚠ **THE TWO PASSES DECIDE ONLY THAT BOUNDARY.** A token the offset is strictly INSIDE can never be one
/// an identifier ends at — spans do not overlap — so scanning for the ending name first cannot take a
/// containing token away from a caret that is genuinely within it.
///
/// ⚠ **A LINEAR SCAN, AND A BINARY SEARCH WOULD BE WRONG.** The lexer records a span only for tokens whose
/// text it sliced — identifiers, keywords, literals, doc comments — so every punctuator, `newline` and EOF
/// carries `startByte == endByte == 0`. The array is therefore NOT ascending in `startByte`, and that same
/// zero span is what correctly makes those tokens unreachable: no offset satisfies `0 <= offset < 0`.
module function lspTokenAtCursor(tokens TokenArray, offset ByteOffset) returns TokenIndex throws LspCursorLookupError
	for (cursor, token) in tokens.withIterator() 'theNameJustTyped'
		if token.kind == TokenKind.identifier and token.endByte == offset 'endsHere'
			return cursor.index()
		end 'endsHere'
	end 'theNameJustTyped'

	// Half-open `[startByte, endByte)`, the interval convention `LspSpan` states.
	for (cursor, token) in tokens.withIterator() 'underTheCursor'
		if token.startByte <= offset and offset < token.endByte 'covers'
			return cursor.index()
		end 'covers'
	end 'underTheCursor'

	throw LspCursorLookupError.nothingThere
end 'lspTokenAtCursor'
